{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat readback/watch-notes.md README.md gen.py plans/tempo-run.json readback/event-184233-as-sent.json'","aggregated_output":"# What the watch showed for event 184233 (Tempo run 45)\n\nIntervals: LTHR 172. Watch (Forerunner 965): auto-detected LTHR 168, zones\nZ1 <118, Z2 118–137, Z3 137–151, Z4 151–162, Z5 >162.\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/184233` returned the event exactly as we sent\nit (see `event-184233-as-sent.json`); `workout_doc.steps` is populated, so the\nAPI accepted it.\n\n| step | planned (% LTHR) | expected on watch (× 172) | watch displayed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | 117–129 | 118–137 |\n| Tempo | 88–93 | 151–160 | 151–162 |\n| Float | 75–82 | 129–141 | 137–151 |\n| Cool-down | 65–72 | 112–124 | 118–137 |\n\nEvery corridor shows up as one of the watch's own zone bands, not the range we\nplanned. Changing the watch's LTHR to 172 moves the bands but they are still\nzone edges, never 151–160 or 129–141. Earlier tries: `units: \"bpm\"` with\nabsolute values made the watch show 214–249 bpm; `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` made\nevery target a constant 101.\n# tri-planner\n\nGenerates structured triathlon workouts and pushes them to Intervals.icu, which\nexports them to the athlete's Garmin watch through Garmin Connect.\n\n- `gen.py` turns a plan (`plans/*.json`) into the body for\n  `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` (an event with `workout_doc`).\n- `push.sh` posts it (needs `INTERVALS_API_KEY` and `ATHLETE_ID`).\n- `readback/` holds what the API returned for the last pushed event and\n  notes on what the watch displayed.\n\nAthlete thresholds live in Intervals (LTHR 172 for this athlete). HR targets\nin plans are percentages of LTHR; Intervals converts them to bpm at export.\n\n```\npython3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json\n./push.sh event.json\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Build an Intervals.icu event body (workout_doc) from a plan file.\n\nUsage: python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json\n\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport sys\n\n\ndef hr_target(step):\n    \"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete's LTHR.\"\"\"\n    lo, hi = step[\"hr_pct\"]\n    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n\n\ndef build_step(step):\n    out = {\"duration\": step[\"minutes\"] * 60}\n    if \"hr_pct\" in step:\n        out[\"hr\"] = hr_target(step)\n    if \"label\" in step:\n        out[\"text\"] = step[\"label\"]\n    return out\n\n\ndef build_doc(plan):\n    steps = []\n    for block in plan[\"steps\"]:\n        if \"repeat\" in block:\n            steps.append({\n                \"reps\": block[\"repeat\"],\n                \"steps\": [build_step(s) for s in block[\"steps\"]],\n            })\n        else:\n            steps.append(build_step(block))\n    return {\"steps\": steps}\n\n\ndef build_event(plan):\n    return {\n        \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n        \"type\": plan[\"sport\"],\n        \"name\": plan[\"name\"],\n        \"start_date_local\": plan[\"date\"] + \"T00:00:00\",\n        \"workout_doc\": build_doc(plan),\n    }\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with open(sys.argv[1]) as f:\n        plan = json.load(f)\n    json.dump(build_event(plan), sys.stdout, indent=2)\n    print()\n{\n  \"name\": \"Tempo run 45\",\n  \"sport\": \"Run\",\n  \"date\": \"2026-10-20\",\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\"label\": \"Warm-up easy\", \"minutes\": 10, \"hr_pct\": [68, 75]},\n    {\"repeat\": 3, \"steps\": [\n      {\"label\": \"Tempo\", \"minutes\": 8, \"hr_pct\": [88, 93]},\n      {\"label\": \"Float\", \"minutes\": 2, \"hr_pct\": [75, 82]}\n    ]},\n    {\"label\": \"Cool-down\", \"minutes\": 5, \"hr_pct\": [65, 72]}\n  ]\n}\n{\n  \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n  \"type\": \"Run\",\n  \"name\": \"Tempo run 45\",\n  \"start_date_local\": \"2026-10-20T00:00:00\",\n  \"workout_doc\": {\n    \"steps\": [\n      {\n        \"duration\": 600,\n        \"hr\": {\n          \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n          \"value\": 68,\n          \"end\": 75\n        },\n        \"text\": \"Warm-up easy\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"reps\": 3,\n        \"steps\": [\n          {\n            \"duration\": 480,\n            \"hr\": {\n              \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n              \"value\": 88,\n              \"end\": 93\n            },\n            \"text\": \"Tempo\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"duration\": 120,\n            \"hr\": {\n              \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n              \"value\": 75,\n              \"end\": 82\n            },\n            \"text\": \"Float\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"duration\": 300,\n        \"hr\": {\n          \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n          \"value\": 65,\n          \"end\": 72\n        },\n        \"text\": \"Cool-down\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md && rg -n 'hr|bpm|lthr|zone|target|Garmin|Intervals' .\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md && rg -n 'hr|bpm|lthr|zone|target|Garmin|Intervals' .\"","aggregated_output":"---\n\n# Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end\n\nMy AI agent generates structured triathlon workouts and pushes them to\nIntervals.icu via `POST /events`; Intervals FIT-exports them to my Garmin\nwatch. The symptom: every HR corridor on the watch was silently wrong —\nclamped to the watch's own (stale, auto-detected) LTHR zones instead of the\nrange we planned. I misdiagnosed it as a watch-side LTHR mismatch for weeks.\n\nWhat we tried, each round-tripped push → API readback → Garmin sync → watch\nface:\n\n1. `{units: \"bpm\", value, end}` — API accepted; watch showed 214–249 bpm\n   (impossible). FIT export misread `bpm` as percent-of-something.\n2. `{start_bpm, end_bpm}` — watch showed a constant 101, Garmin's \"no valid\n   HR target\" fallback. Schema silently dropped.\n3. Surveyed the Intervals UI target dropdown, concluded \"absolute bpm is\n   impossible\". Wrong — that dropdown picks the units label, not the value\n   shape.\n\nThe breakthrough: build a workout in the Intervals **UI** with its\n\"Instant HR\" toggle, then read it back via the API. The corridor's lower\nbound is `start`, not `value`. That one key name switches the FIT export\nmode: `{value, end}` routes as a **Lap-HR / zone-mapped** target (the watch\nclamps it to its local zones), while `{start, end}` becomes an **Instant-HR\ncorridor** — the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed. Verified\nend-to-end: Intervals LTHR 172, watch LTHR 168, a 75–82% target rendered\n129–141 bpm on the watch — matching Intervals, drift-immune. The fix was a\none-word rename after days of probing.\n\nBonus trap from the same session: steps were invisible in the Intervals web\nUI, because the UI reads a separate top-level `description` in Intervals'\ntext grammar — where `m` means *minutes* (`200m` = a 200-minute step!),\ndistance must be `mtr`/`km`, a bare `Z2` resolves to *power* zones even for\nswims, and a digit at the start of a label gets eaten as a duration. If the\nparser chokes, Intervals wipes both representations.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: an Intervals.icu account with API access (OAuth token or API\nkey), athlete thresholds configured in Intervals (LTHR at minimum), and a\nGarmin watch that syncs workouts from Intervals via Garmin Connect.\n\nYour agent will need from you: your Intervals athlete id and API credential;\nyour LTHR as stored in Intervals; permission to create (and later delete) a\ntest calendar event.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create an event via `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` with\n   `workout_doc.steps`, where every HR step target is\n   `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": <low>, \"end\": <high>}` — never `value`.\n   Percentages, not absolute bpm: Intervals converts at FIT-export time from\n   its own stored threshold.\n2. Set the event's top-level `description` in Intervals' native text grammar\n   so steps render in the web UI, e.g. `- Warm-up easy 10m 75-82% LTHR`.\n   Rules: durations `m`/`s`, distance `mtr`/`km`, blank lines around `Nx`\n   repeat blocks, no digits at the start of labels, no bare `Z<number>`\n   outside cycling.\n3. Read the event back with `GET /events/{id}`: non-empty `workout_doc.steps`\n   means the parser accepted the description; `steps: []` means it choked.\n4. Sync the watch through Garmin Connect and open the scheduled workout.\n\nVerify: the watch shows a bpm corridor equal to your percentages × the LTHR\nstored in Intervals — not the watch's own LTHR (easiest to prove when the two\ndiffer). The Intervals web UI shows the full step chart instead of just a\nname and total duration.\n---\n\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\n\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\ndatabase, no build step.\n\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\n\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\nconfusion.\n\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\n\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\nthe email are the confirmation, not the fetch.\n\nSimple things should stay simple.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a static site you can edit and deploy, and a Google account.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which spreadsheet to write into (or permission\nto create one), the address to notify, and the list of fields to collect.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create a Google Sheet for submissions. Its ID is the long string in the URL\n   between `/d/` and `/edit`.\n2. In that sheet choose Extensions, then Apps Script. Write a `doPost(e)` that\n   parses `e.postData.contents` as JSON; returns success immediately and writes\n   nothing if the honeypot field is non-empty; rejects missing required fields;\n   takes a `LockService.getScriptLock()` before writing so two submissions in\n   the same second cannot interleave; appends a row via\n   `SpreadsheetApp.openById(<id>).getSheets()[0].appendRow([...])` with a\n   timestamp first; releases the lock; then sends a notification with\n   `MailApp.sendEmail`. Truncate every field to a maximum length before writing.\n3. Deploy, New deployment, type \"Web app\", Execute as **Me**, Who has access\n   **Anyone**. Authorise it. Copy the URL ending in `/exec`.\n4. In the page, add the form with the real fields, plus a hidden input that no\n   human can see or tab into (`tabindex=\"-1\"`, `aria-hidden=\"true\"`) as the\n   honeypot, and a status element with `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"`.\n5. Validate in the browser first, setting `aria-invalid` on each field and\n   focusing the first bad one, so a screen reader announces the problem.\n6. Submit with `fetch(url, { method: \"POST\", mode: \"no-cors\", headers: {\n   \"Content-Type\": \"text/plain;charset=utf-8\" }, body: JSON.stringify(data) })`.\n   Do not use `application/json`: it triggers a preflight that Apps Script\n   never answers. Because the response is opaque, treat the resolved promise as\n   success and let the sheet and the email be the source of truth.\n7. Read `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign` from the query string, fall\n   back to `document.referrer`, and send that as a `source` field.\n8. When you later change the script, use Deploy, Manage deployments, the pencil\n   icon, and set Version to \"New version\". A fresh deployment would give you a\n   different `/exec` URL and silently break the live form.\n\nVerify: submit the form on the deployed site. A row appears in the sheet within\na few seconds and the notification email arrives. Then fill the honeypot field\nfrom devtools and submit again: no new row appears, and the endpoint still\nreports success, so a bot sees nothing unusual.\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `<path>` instead — guaranteed\n  everywhere.\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\n\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\nclick-to-copy version inside the app too, but the embed is just markdown, so it\nworks anywhere markdown does. The badges are already on my own repo's README,\ndoing their little double duty.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a web app/API you control that stores a public, per-item stat\n(reproductions, stars, downloads, streak — anything already public), and a way\nto add a public HTTP route.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which stat to surface, where it lives (your DB/\nstore), your brand colors, and your public base URL.\n\nSteps:\n1. Add a **public** `GET /badge/{id}.svg` route that looks up the item's stat.\n   No auth — the badge must be embeddable by anyone; only expose already-public\n   numbers.\n2. Return a two-segment \"shields-style\" SVG: a dark label segment (your app\n   name) + a colored value segment (the stat). Compute each segment's width from\n   its text length so it never clips. Set `Content-Type: image/svg+xml`.\n3. Draw any icon (✓, ★) as an inline `<path>`, never a unicode character — text\n   glyphs depend on the viewer's fonts and show as boxes in some renderers. Keep\n   the SVG fully self-contained (no external fonts or images).\n4. Add a short `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=300`). GitHub's image\n   proxy will cache it regardless; this just keeps the source cheap.\n5. Hand users the embed line — the trailing link is the backlink that promotes\n   your app:\n   `[![yourapp](https://yourapp.com/badge/{id}.svg)](https://yourapp.com/item/{id})`\n6. Optional: show the same embed as click-to-copy in your app's UI so users find\n   it without reading docs.\n\nVerify: paste the badge into a GitHub README. It must render as a crisp badge —\nnot a broken-image icon, and no `□` where the icon should be. Confirm the number\nmatches your app. Then bump the item's stat and confirm the badge updates after\nGitHub's cache window (a README edit busts it immediately).\n\nCaveat to state up front: because of GitHub's camo cache, treat the count as\n\"eventually fresh,\" not real-time — and keep the SVG self-contained, or camo\nwill render a broken image.\n./plans/tempo-run.json:6:    {\"label\": \"Warm-up easy\", \"minutes\": 10, \"hr_pct\": [68, 75]},\n./plans/tempo-run.json:8:      {\"label\": \"Tempo\", \"minutes\": 8, \"hr_pct\": [88, 93]},\n./plans/tempo-run.json:9:      {\"label\": \"Float\", \"minutes\": 2, \"hr_pct\": [75, 82]}\n./plans/tempo-run.json:11:    {\"label\": \"Cool-down\", \"minutes\": 5, \"hr_pct\": [65, 72]}\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:10:        \"hr\": {\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:11:          \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:22:            \"hr\": {\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:23:              \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:31:            \"hr\": {\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:32:              \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:42:        \"hr\": {\n./readback/event-184233-as-sent.json:43:          \"units\": \"%lthr\",\n./gen.py:2:\"\"\"Build an Intervals.icu event body (workout_doc) from a plan file.\n./gen.py:10:def hr_target(step):\n./gen.py:12:    lo, hi = step[\"hr_pct\"]\n./gen.py:13:    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n./gen.py:18:    if \"hr_pct\" in step:\n./gen.py:19:        out[\"hr\"] = hr_target(step)\n./readback/watch-notes.md:3:Intervals: LTHR 172. Watch (Forerunner 965): auto-detected LTHR 168, zones\n./readback/watch-notes.md:17:Every corridor shows up as one of the watch's own zone bands, not the range we\n./readback/watch-notes.md:19:zone edges, never 151–160 or 129–141. Earlier tries: `units: \"bpm\"` with\n./readback/watch-notes.md:20:absolute values made the watch show 214–249 bpm; `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` made\n./readback/watch-notes.md:21:every target a constant 101.\n./README.md:3:Generates structured triathlon workouts and pushes them to Intervals.icu, which\n./README.md:4:exports them to the athlete's Garmin watch through Garmin Connect.\n./README.md:12:Athlete thresholds live in Intervals (LTHR 172 for this athlete). HR targets\n./README.md:13:in plans are percentages of LTHR; Intervals converts them to bpm at export.\n./notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md:14:bill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\n./notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md:30:request and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:3:# Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:6:Intervals.icu via `POST /events`; Intervals FIT-exports them to my Garmin\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:8:clamped to the watch's own (stale, auto-detected) LTHR zones instead of the\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:11:What we tried, each round-tripped push → API readback → Garmin sync → watch\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:14:1. `{units: \"bpm\", value, end}` — API accepted; watch showed 214–249 bpm\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:15:   (impossible). FIT export misread `bpm` as percent-of-something.\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:16:2. `{start_bpm, end_bpm}` — watch showed a constant 101, Garmin's \"no valid\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:17:   HR target\" fallback. Schema silently dropped.\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:18:3. Surveyed the Intervals UI target dropdown, concluded \"absolute bpm is\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:22:The breakthrough: build a workout in the Intervals **UI** with its\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:25:mode: `{value, end}` routes as a **Lap-HR / zone-mapped** target (the watch\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:26:clamps it to its local zones), while `{start, end}` becomes an **Instant-HR\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:27:corridor** — the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed. Verified\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:28:end-to-end: Intervals LTHR 172, watch LTHR 168, a 75–82% target rendered\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:29:129–141 bpm on the watch — matching Intervals, drift-immune. The fix was a\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:32:Bonus trap from the same session: steps were invisible in the Intervals web\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:33:UI, because the UI reads a separate top-level `description` in Intervals'\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:35:distance must be `mtr`/`km`, a bare `Z2` resolves to *power* zones even for\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:37:parser chokes, Intervals wipes both representations.\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:41:Prerequisites: an Intervals.icu account with API access (OAuth token or API\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:42:key), athlete thresholds configured in Intervals (LTHR at minimum), and a\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:43:Garmin watch that syncs workouts from Intervals via Garmin Connect.\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:45:Your agent will need from you: your Intervals athlete id and API credential;\n./notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md:46:your LTHR as stored in Intervals; permission to create (and later delete) a\n./notes/exact-hr